\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Yerakai (also spelled Yerekai) is spoken in Yerakai ward, Ambunti Rural LLG, northern Papua New Guinea. Wikipedia and Ethnologue place it in the Sepik family. Glottolog finds no significant lexical relationship to other Sepik languages and classifies it as an isolate. Wikipedia reports 380 speakers (2000 census). Joshua Project reports 700 for the people group, which is a different measure. Glottolog gives its vitality as ``shifting''.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Yerakai turned up in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia on Yerakai: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yerakai_language}
\item Glottolog entry for Yerakai: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/yera1243}
\item Glottolog entry for the Sepik family: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/sepi1257}
\item Wikipedia on the Middle Sepik languages: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Sepik_languages}
\end{itemize}
The search summary also mentions a PARADISEC collection of Don Laycock materials that includes Yerakai. No URL for it appeared in the results, so none is given here.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Yerakai in Papua New Guinea: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/16026}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No openly accessible audio or digital resources were identified.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Yerakai is small and shifting, and no school materials were found. Any curriculum work would depend on archival data (such as the Laycock materials) and on new documentation done with the community.
\end{document}
